<div id="hint">Drag to spin · click the planet to land</div>
```

## 4. Data

```js
const SHEET = {w: 2336, h: 1744};
// map position u,v (0..1 from top-left), card crop x,y,w,h on the 2336x1744 sheet
const SITES = [
  {name: 'Olympus Mons', u: .121, v: .31, box: [0, 0, 1150, 810]},
  {name: 'Valles Marineris', u: .45, v: .56, box: [1186, 0, 1150, 810]},
  {name: 'Gale Crater', u: .85, v: .75, box: [0, 900, 1150, 810]},
  {name: 'Jezero Delta', u: .61, v: .35, box: [1186, 900, 1150, 810]}
];
const THARSIS = {u: .15, v: .41};
```
Direction of a map point (matches `SphereGeometry` uv): `ph = 2π·u`, `th = π·v`, `dir = (-cos ph·sin th, cos th, sin ph·sin th)`. To face a point at the camera: `faceYaw = atan2(-d.x, d.z)`, `faceLat = asin(d.y)`.

`#sites` renders `<li data-i>` per site. `pick(i)`: set `.on` on that item, set the card crop, move the card vertically so its centre aligns with the item (`rect.top + rect.height/2 - card.offsetHeight/2`), and set `#destName`. Hover calls `pick(i)`. Clicking an item calls `pick(i); go(2)`. `#explore` calls `go(2)`, `#home` calls `go(0)`, rail dots call `go(i)`.

## 5. Three.js scene

`WebGLRenderer({canvas,alpha:true,antialias:true})`, pixel ratio `min(dpr,2)`. **OrthographicCamera** in CSS pixels (`left=-W/2 … top=H/2`, near −2e5, far 2e5), so the planet can grow arbitrarily large. Hierarchy `planet` (position in px, scale = radius) > `tilt` (rotation.x = pitch) > `spin` (rotation.y = yaw) > mesh.

`planet.position = (cx - W/2, H/2 - cy, 0)`.

Sphere: `SphereGeometry(1,128,96)` with this ShaderMaterial. Uniforms: `map`, `ready`, `texel=(1/2688,1/1344)`, `sun=normalize(.62,.5,.6)`, `hiDir`, `hi`, `hiR`.

```glsl
// vertex
  varying vec2 vUv; varying vec3 vObj; varying vec3 vN;
  void main(){ vUv = uv; vObj = normalize(position); vN = normalize(mat3(modelMatrix) * normal);
    gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.); }
// fragment
    uniform sampler2D map; uniform vec2 texel; uniform vec3 sun, hiDir; uniform float hi, hiR, ready;
    varying vec2 vUv; varying vec3 vObj; varying vec3 vN;
    float luma(vec2 u){ return dot(texture2D(map, u).rgb, vec3(.3,.6,.1)); }
    void main(){
      vec3 col = texture2D(map, vUv).rgb; col = pow(col, vec3(2.6)) * vec3(1.18,.74,.56);
      // relief from the map's own brightness
      vec3 n = normalize(vN);
      vec3 t = normalize(cross(vec3(0.,1.,0.), n)), b = cross(n, t);
      float dx = luma(vUv + vec2(texel.x*2.,0.)) - luma(vUv - vec2(texel.x*2.,0.));
      float dy = luma(vUv + vec2(0.,texel.y*2.)) - luma(vUv - vec2(0.,texel.y*2.));
      n = normalize(n - (t*dx + b*dy) * 1.9);
      float diff = max(dot(n, sun), 0.);
      vec3 lit = col * (vec3(1.5,1.32,1.18) * pow(diff, .85) + vec3(.2,.09,.06));
      // highlighted region with a rough edge, like a territory on a map
      float d = acos(clamp(dot(normalize(vObj), hiDir), -1., 1.)) + (luma(vUv) - .45) * .22;
      float m = smoothstep(hiR, hiR - .03, d), edge = smoothstep(.03, .0, abs(d - hiR + .01));
      lit = mix(lit, mix(lit, vec3(.62,.012,.07) * (.25 + diff * 1.05) * (.5 + luma(vUv) * 1.1), .9), m * hi) + vec3(1.,.42,.4) * edge * hi * .22;
      float fres = pow(1. - max(vN.z, 0.), 3.2);
      lit += vec3(1.,.5,.26) * fres * (.05 + diff * .5);
      lit = lit / (lit * .3 + 1.) * 1.3;
      gl_FragColor = vec4(pow(lit, vec3(1./2.2)), 1.) * ready;
    }
```

Atmosphere: `SphereGeometry(1.14,64,48)` added to `planet` (not spun), `BackSide`, additive, transparent, no depth write:

```glsl
// vertex
varying vec3 vN; void main(){ vN = normalize(mat3(modelMatrix)*normal); gl_Position = projectionMatrix*modelViewMatrix*vec4(position,1.); }
// fragment
uniform vec3 sun; uniform float ready; varying vec3 vN;
    void main(){ float z = clamp(-vN.z, 0., 1.), a = smoothstep(.0, .55, z);
      float s = .55 + .6 * max(dot(-vN * vec3(1.,1.,0.), normalize(sun * vec3(1.,1.,0.))), 0.);
      a = pow(a, 2.6) * s * .7; gl_FragColor = vec4(vec3(1.,.64,.36), a * ready); }
```

## 6. Layout

`R0 = portrait ? W*.36 : min(H*.3, W*.19)`, with `portrait = H > W*1.05`.

## 7. State flow

`st` runs 0..2. `go(n)`: ignore if `n === to`; `from = st; to = n; t0 = now`. Duration is the sum of the spans crossed: 1300ms for 0↔1, 2500ms for 1↔2 (1ms when reduced motion). `st = mix(from,to,io(seg(now,t0,t0+dur)))`, with `a = clamp(st)` and `b = clamp(st-1)`.

Triggers: wheel `deltaY` beyond ±8 steps `to` ±1 (locked 1400ms); Escape steps back; clicking the planet (pointer moved <6px, inside the radius, `to<2`) advances.

Helpers: `seg(p,a,b)=clamp((p-a)/(b-a))`; `io(k)=k<.5?4k³:1-(-2k+2)³/2`; `out(k)=1-(1-k)³`.

Per frame:

**Intro** (`age` = seconds since the map loaded): `grow = out(seg(age,0,1.5))`, `iw = out(seg(age,.75,1.7))`.

**Planet:** `bz = io(seg(b,0,.62))`.
- `px = portrait ? .5W : mix(.5W,.36W,a)`
- `py = portrait ? mix(.4H,.33H,a) : .5H`
- `R = R0 * mix(.03,1,grow) * (1+a*.04) * exp(bz*2.5)`
- `cx = mix(px,.5W,bz)`, `cy = mix(py,.5H,bz)`

**Rotation:** start `yaw = faceYaw(dir(u .4, v .5))`, `pitch = .16`. Auto-spin at `st<.02`, no drag, 1.5s after the last drag: `yaw += dt*(.08+(1-grow)*2.4)`. Drag: `k = 1.1/R0`, `yaw += dx*k`, `pitch = clamp(pitch+dy*k,-1,1)`; inertia `yaw += vy; vy *= exp(-dt*4)`. When `st>.02`, steer to the target (Tharsis, or the site once `b>0`): shortest-arc yaw ease at rate 3.6, `pitch` to `faceLat` at rate 3.6. Back at rest, `pitch` returns to .16 at rate 1.2. Ease factor `f(r)=1-exp(-dt*r)`, `dt` capped at .05.

**Highlight:** `hiDir` = site when `b>.02`, else Tharsis. `hiR = b>.02 ? mix(.62,.3,seg(b,0,.1)) : .62`. `hi = clamp(seg(a,.15,.6)+(b>0?1:0))`.

**word0:** translate `-50%` plus `-a·.08W` in x (0 in portrait); y `-50%` plus `mouseY*-6`, where `mouseY` is pointer y/H − .5. In portrait y offset is `H*-.1 + R*1.08 + wordHeight*.5`, so the word sits under the planet. `clip-path: inset(-10% I% -10% I%)` with `I=(1-iw)*50`, so it opens from the centre. Opacity `1-seg(a,.1,.5)`.

**eyebrow:** left-aligned to the word's left edge + 2% of word width, `wordHeight/2 + 34` above centre, moves with the word. In portrait centred below the word. Opacity `iw*(1-seg(a,.05,.35))`.

**flare:** at `(cx+R*.74, cy-R*.62)`, opacity `grow*(.88+.12 sin(t*1.3))*(1-seg(bz,0,.35))`.

**hint:** opacity `iw*.8*(1-seg(a,0,.25))`.

**pin:** projects Tharsis: `dir` rotated by spin then tilt. Screen position `(cx+x*R-6, cy-y*R-8)`. Opacity `seg(z,.22,.42)*seg(age,1.6,2.1)*(1-seg(a,0,.3))`.

**word1:** `ka=seg(a,.45,1)`, opacity `ka*(1-seg(b,.02,.16))`, x `-50%` plus `.02W+(1-out(ka))*40` (0 in portrait), y `.56H` (portrait `.46H`).

**sites + card:** opacity `seg(a,.55,1)*(1-seg(b,0,.14))`, hidden when ≈0, no pointer events under .6. Card translateX `(1-out(seg(a,.6,1)))*26`.

**#gl** takes pointer events only while `st<1.02`.

**region** (site name, uppercased, centred on the zoomed planet): opacity `seg(b,.14,.26)*(1-seg(b,.42,.52))`.

**fog:** `fi=seg(b,.36,.54)`, `fo=seg(b,.62,.96)`, opacity `fi*(1-fo)`.

**Landing layers** visible when `b>.5`. `lk=out(seg(b,.5,1))`, `fk=out(seg(b,.58,1))`, `dk=out(seg(b,.66,.98))`, `drift=1+.02 sin(t*.18)`, `mouseX` = pointer x/W − .5:
- land: `translate(mouseX*-14, mix(.2H, mouseY*-8, lk)) scale(mix(1.3,1.05,lk)*drift)`
- fore: `translate(mouseX*-46, mix(.42H,0,fk)) scale(1.06)`
- dest: `translate(mouseX*-26, mix(.62H, .27H, dk))` (portrait end `.3H`); its `small` opacity `seg(b,.8,1)*.92`

**bar:** `bk=seg(b,.86,1)`, opacity bk, `translate(-50%,(1-out(bk))*34px)`.

**scrim:** opacity `seg(b,.6,.9)`, so the nav stays legible over the bright sky.

**rail:** `.on` on the dot at `round(st)`.

## 8. Dust

`#dust` is a 2D canvas (pixel ratio `min(dpr,1.5)`). Pre-render a 64px radial sprite: `rgba(255,214,170,.95)` → `rgba(255,180,120,.42)` at .4 → transparent. 34 motes with seeded random `x,y∈0..1`, `r=3+rnd²*34`, `s=.004+rnd*.012`, `p=rnd*6.28`. Each frame: `x=((m.x+t*m.s+2)%1.1-.05)*W`, `y=(m.y+sin(t*.3+m.p)*.02)*H`, alpha `.3+.35*sin(t*.7+m.p)²` (×.7 on the landing).

## 9. Feel

A premium travel site that happens to sell another planet. Heavy white type, one vivid planet with real weight in the hand, and a single unbroken camera move from orbit to the ground. Keep it all warm rust and orange, with crimson for the region and the call to action.

items.prompt_text:
# Video Ribbon — Vante Scroll Portfolio Hero (exact recreation prompt)

Build a single-file, full-viewport, scroll-driven portfolio hero called **Vante**. On a near-black page, the two-part headline `CRAFT IN` / `MOTION` slides across the screen in opposite directions while a continuous ribbon of live video cards spirals around the viewer in 3D. It starts small along the bottom, swings close in a shallow curved arc, wraps around the left edge, runs away along the top and leaves top right. Then the cards land one by one in a calm 3×2 grid.

Plain HTML, CSS and one vanilla ES module in one file. The only external resources are the Google Fonts, three.js 0.170.0 from jsDelivr and the eight video URLs listed below. Reproduce every value, string and timing exactly. The CSS, markup and script are given verbatim, so copy them without changes.

Four rules define it:
1. **Everything comes from one number.** Scroll progress `p` from 0 to 1, smoothed with `p += (target - p) * .09` each frame, drives everything. Scrolling back reverses it perfectly.
2. **The ribbon runs on a fixed rail.** The path never moves. The ribbon only slides along it, card after card.
3. **Text always reads correctly.** The cards are a two-sided strip. When a card is seen from behind on the near pass, the fragment shader flips `uv.x`, so videos are never mirrored.
4. **The curve stays gentle up close.** The near pass is a shallow arc, about 5.4 to 7.6 units from the camera, so the middle card stays wide and flat. The far runs reach 24 units away, so the cards look tiny there.

---

## 1. Page basics

- Title: `Vante — Craft in Motion`
- Meta description: `A studio portfolio where a ribbon of live video work sweeps through space, then lands in a grid.`
- Fonts: `https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Inter+Tight:wght@400;500;600&display=swap`, with preconnects to fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com (crossorigin).
- Import map: `three` → `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js`
- Colours: background `#0a0a0b`, ink `#f4f1ea`, mid `#8d8b86`, line `rgba(244,241,234,.14)`.
- Structure: `#track` is 900vh tall and holds a sticky 100vh `.stage`.

## 2. Videos (use these exact URLs)

All are hosted with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and are loaded with `crossOrigin="anonymous"`, muted, looping and inline. Each one is a `THREE.VideoTexture`.

1. Roadster 3D: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/designs/roadster-3d-car-landing.mp4`
2. Living Portrait: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/aaaf/living-portrait-hero.mp4`
3. Particle Self: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/aaaf/oraciteslArea.mp4`
4. Robot Jellyfish: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/aaaf/medusa-jellyfish-hero.mp4`
5. Ink Reveal: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/designs/ink-reveal-hover.mp4`
6. Space Voyage: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/designs/space-voyage-video.mp4`
7. Helion Dispatch: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/designs/helion-dispatch-landing.mp4`
8. Honey Bee: `https://pub-86dc5b5484314368ac5436a674b0d919.r2.dev/designs/honey-bee-melli-landing.mp4`

The first six land in the grid. The last two appear only in the ribbon. Videos play only while their card is visible.

## 3. Layout

- **Top left:** a small triangular mark and `VANTE`, then two boxed pager arrows `←` and `→` hanging from the top edge.
- **Top centre:** `START A PROJECT →` in IBM Plex Mono with an underline.
- **Top right:** a `LET'S TALK` pill with a dot and a `MENU` pill with a two-line icon.
- **Bottom left:** `Concepts, explorations and interface experiments shared openly as part of our creative process.`
- **Bottom centre:** `VISUALS / FILM / MUSIC / EXPERIMENTS`. **Bottom right:** `VIEW SHOWREEL` with a short line.
- **Centre:** the caption `Exploring ideas through daily design practice.`, small and uppercase, between the two headline lines.
- **Headline:** Inter Tight 400, `clamp(40px,6.7vw,130px)`, letter-spacing `-.055em`. The two lines are offset horizontally and stack about one line apart.

## 4. First frame

At `p = 0` the screen looks almost empty and technical. A soft lighter glow fades down from the top over 42% of the height. A hairline with a small `+` at its centre crosses at 37.5% of the height. Only the end of the headline shows: `IN` at the far left edge and the start of `M` at the far right edge, both low on the screen, with the caption low in the centre. The glow, hairline, pager and `START A PROJECT` link fade out by `p = .05`.

## 5. Scroll timeline

| Progress | What happens |
|---|---|
| 0–.12 | Headline and caption rise 34vh into place |
| 0–.58 | `CRAFT IN` runs from -50vw to +76vw, `MOTION` runs the opposite way, crossing near .24 |
| .14–.63 | Ribbon slides along the whole rail |
| .52–.57 | Caption fades |
| As each card leaves the rail | That card flies straight to its grid slot over .13 of scroll, so there is never an empty screen |
| .72–.86 | A hairline draws across between the two rows |

A card is on the rail until it reaches the end of it, then it switches to its grid pose. Each card flies in from its own side with a small tilt, eased with `1-(1-t)^3`. Top row cards come from above, bottom row cards from below, outer cards from the sides.

## 6. The ribbon rail

Built from 161 sampled points as a centripetal `CatmullRomCurve3`, read back by arc length into a table of 1201 points and tangents. For `t` in 0–1 and `ph` from `-1.5` to `2π + 1.35`:

- `cs = cos(ph)`
- `depth = cs > 0 ? 7.6 + 16.4 * cs^.9 : 7.6 + 2.2 * cs`
- `edge = .84 + .5 * span(.14 - t, 0, .14) + .8 * span(t, .84, 1)`
- `u = 2t - 1`
- Point: `(edge * sin(ph) * depth * .561, .5 * sign(u) * |u|^1.4 * depth * .63, -depth)`

Cards are 2.1 × 1.32 units with a gap of .03, so they read as one strip. Each is a `PlaneGeometry` with 28 × 3 segments whose vertices are rewritten every frame by walking the rail. The local up vector is world Y made perpendicular to the tangent.

Camera: field of view 35°. Below 16:9 the field of view is recalculated with `atan(.561 / aspect)` so the horizontal view stays fixed and the ribbon is never squashed on a narrower window.

## 7. Card shader

A `ShaderMaterial` with an analytic rounded rectangle (`radius .018`), a cover fit from the video's aspect ratio, a dark placeholder of `vec3(.075)` until the video loads, a faint light edge, and `if(!gl_FrontFacing) uv.x = 1. - uv.x`. Output colour space is linear passthrough, with texture colour space `NoColorSpace`, so the videos keep their true colours.

## 8. Grid

Three columns and two rows on a plane at z = -8, 74% of the view width, gap 1.7% of that width. Below an aspect of .9 it becomes two columns and three rows at 88% width. Past `p = .86`, hovering a card shows a small ink pill with the design name that follows the cursor.

